   "feed_anfang": "Vector search that looks cheap at ten million embeddings can turn into a RAM budget crisis at a billion. This episode is for Azure and AI architects building RAG and enterprise search on Azure AI Search, Cosmos DB or SQL Server. You get a practical framework for choosing between HNSW and DiskANN based on scale, update frequency, latency and cost.<br /><br /><b>In this episode</b><br /><ul><li>00:00 – Why the HNSW default becomes a budget problem</li><li>04:07 – Vector search and approximate nearest neighbor basics</li><li>07:24 – HNSW explained and the memory wall</li><li>13:39 – DiskANN architecture and production numbers</li><li>20:15 – DiskANN in Azure and the four-variable decision framework</li><li>27:19 – RAM vs. SSD cost model and index mutability</li><li>33:34 – RAG pipelines, failure modes, metadata filtering and multi-tenancy</li><li>54:03 – Evaluation, observability, roadmap and the decision guide</li></ul><br /><b>Key takeaways</b><br /><ul><li>HNSW keeps the whole graph in RAM and is the right choice for smaller corpora with strict latency needs, but at roughly a billion vectors it needs terabytes of memory per replica.</li><li>DiskANN keeps a compressed navigation layer in RAM and the full graph on SSD, reaching similar recall at a fraction of the memory, with <a href=\"https://www.m365.fm/entity/cosmos-db/?utm_source=podcast&utm_medium=shownotes&utm_campaign=the-billion-vector-problem-hnsw-vs-diskann-in-azure-ai-search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">",
